<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Selim II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1524">1524</a>–<a href="/years/1574">1574</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Selim the Blond, Sarı Selim, Selim the Drunkard · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-dynasty">Ottoman dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Selim II was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from 1566 until his death in 1574, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurrem-sultan">Hürrem Sultan</a>. He secured the succession by defeating his brother Bayezid near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in 1559; Bayezid fled to Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and was handed over and executed with his four sons in 1561. Selim did not lead his armies in person, and his grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokollu-mehmed-pasha">Sokollu Mehmed Pasha</a> conducted much of the government. His reign saw the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1570–1571, a heavy naval defeat at Lepanto in 1571, the recovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1574 and the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selimiye-mosque">Selimiye Mosque</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>. He died after a fall on a marble floor while drunk.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/selim-ii.jpg" alt="Selim II" width="330" height="570" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nakkaş Osman, Şemâ'ilnâme, 1579, Istanbul, Topkapı Sarayı Müzesi, Н. 1563. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Selim_II_by_Nakkaş_Osman,_Şemâ'ilnâme,_1579,_Istanbul,_Topkapı_Sarayı_Müzesi,_Н._1563.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Selim was born on 28 May 1524 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, the second surviving son of Suleiman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurrem-sultan">Hürrem Sultan</a>. In 1542 he became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> as his seat, and after the death of his brother Şehzade Mehmed in November 1543 he was appointed governor of Saruhan, residing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a>. The execution of his elder half-brother Şehzade Mustafa on Suleiman's order in September 1553 left Selim and his brother Bayezid as the main contenders for the throne. Their rivalry ended when Bayezid marched on Konya in 1559 and Selim, with troops sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokollu-mehmed-pasha">Sokollu Mehmed Pasha</a>, won the engagement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Selim took the throne on 29 September 1566. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokollu-mehmed-pasha">Sokollu Mehmed Pasha</a> kept Suleiman's death secret until Selim reached the army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, where the allegiance ceremony was held. The soldiers, refused an accession payment, revolted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> until Selim's sister Mihrimah Sultan paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> from her own fortune. Sokollu, grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> from 1565 until his assassination in 1579, persuaded Selim to make peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a> of 1568 had Maximilian II recognise Ottoman conquests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and keep paying tribute. Sokollu opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> campaign, but Selim ordered it.</p><p>Elsewhere the reign met mixed fortunes. In 1567 the Zaydi imam al-Mutahhar ibn Yahya Sharaf al-Din rose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaa">Sana'a</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiz">Ta'izz</a>; Ottoman forces recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, Ta'izz and much of the highlands, and a peace at Kawkaban in May 1570 brought recognition of Ottoman suzerainty. A fleet intended for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>, which had asked for help against the Portuguese, was diverted to Yemen, and only two ships with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and gunsmiths arrived. An attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a> in 1569 failed, and a canal between the Don and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> was left unfinished. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> also pressed for Henry of Valois's election in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1573. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in a peace of 7 March 1573 and agreed to pay 300,000 ducats.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Selim died on 15 December 1574. His wife Nurbanu Sultan kept the death hidden for several days so that her son Murad could arrive and claim the throne unopposed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-iii">Murad III</a> later had five of Selim's young sons executed. Selim was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>. He wrote poetry under the pen name Selimi, supported poets, and restored the stipend of Mahidevran, mother of Mustafa. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selimiye-mosque">Selimiye Mosque</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>, built by Sinan between 1569 and 1575, is the largest of all Ottoman mosques, and Sinan also renovated Hagia Sophia between 1572 and 1574.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Selim II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Suleiman's own mausoleum was built after his death on the orders of his son and successor <b>Selim II</b>, between 1566 and 1568.</blockquote><a href="/work/suleymaniye-mosque">Süleymaniye Mosque · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>A full Janissary lived in barracks, drew his pay in cash, could not marry until <b>Selim II</b> allowed it in 1566, and in peacetime policed Istanbul and fought its fires; only his own officers could punish him.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.96&amp;year=1556" aria-label="Istanbul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5258 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Selim II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5708 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5708 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.96&amp;year=1556">What was here in 1556? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
